#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""SIGedge -- ka9q-radio-builder

20260831-0000

Standalone Textual TUI for browsing existing ka9q-radio radiod configs and
building new ones from them, styled after ka9q-radio's own `control`
program: bordered panels and a cursor-driven, single-letter-hotkey editor
(see source/ka9q-radio/docs/utils/control.md for that program's own key
table).

Two-pane left column, one read-only and one writable, each a small file
browser (Enter on a "name/" row descends, Enter on ".." goes back up),
feeding a shared config editor on the right:

  - Source (upper-left, read-only) -- browses whatever directory of
    existing radiod@<instance>.conf files is currently selected ('o' to
    jump straight to one, or Enter/'..' to navigate there). Starts
    pointed at /usr/share/ka9q-radio/examples, the vendor example configs
    installed by the ka9q-radio packages, referenced throughout
    source/ka9q-radio/docs/SDR/*.md; the live mission directory
    (/etc/radio, overridable via $KA9Q_CONFIG_DIR) is the other one-click
    preset. Browsing is unconfined -- '..' works all the way to /. This
    app never opens anything under Source for writing -- highlighting a
    conf-file row only loads a *copy* into the editor.
  - Destination (lower-left, writable) -- the operator's own scratch
    space, starting at /tmp but, like Source, freely navigable anywhere
    with Enter/'..'. 'w' Write always lands in whatever Destination
    directory is currently browsed, as radiod@<instance>.conf, whether
    the editor's content started from a Source file or from 'n' New --
    so editing an example and writing it never overwrites the example
    itself, wherever Destination happens to be pointed. 'x' Delete
    (Destination) removes a file there the same way; 's'/'t' toggle the
    real radiod@<instance>.service matching whatever's selected in
    Destination (a note is logged if /etc/radio has no matching config,
    since that's what radiod itself actually reads, wherever Destination
    is currently pointed). None of this needs or uses sudo -- every
    Destination write/delete runs with the filesystem permissions of
    whoever launched this script, exactly like any other program they
    run; navigate somewhere they can't write and the OS simply refuses
    it, reported the same way any other failed write here is.

The editor pane (right, full height) parses each config with Python's
configparser (preserving section/key order and exact key case) and
renders it as a Tree: mission -> sections -> key/value leaves, with
add/change/delete ('a'/'c'/'x') applied immediately to the in-memory copy
-- nothing reaches disk until 'w' Write, and that always targets
Destination, wherever it's currently browsing.

Design note -- earlier versions of this tool wrote configs straight into
/etc/radio via `sudo tee` (mirroring scripts/cfg_ka9q-radio's own
backup_config convention). That's gone: every save is now a plain,
unprivileged write under whatever directory Destination is currently
browsing (starting at /tmp), so building/trying a config here never
risks the live mission set through elevated privilege -- though nothing
stops the operator from deliberately navigating Destination to a live
directory they otherwise have write access to and saving there directly.
cfg_ka9q-radio is untouched and remains the non-interactive generator
setup_services uses for the three reference templates; this tool is for
genuine add/change/delete/update over the complete config surface --
including starting from any vendor example -- without cfg_ka9q-radio's
fixed single-channel template limits.

Advanced settings ('v') -- a curated picklist, offered against whatever
section the cursor is on in the editor, classified into one of three
catalogs by comparing the section name against [global]'s "hardware ="
value (the same test radiod itself uses, see docs/ka9q-radio-2.md):
  - [global] itself -> GLOBAL_ADVANCED_SETTINGS: less-common global-only
    keys (ttl, tos, blocktime, overlap, fft-threads, dns, rtcp, sap,
    mode-file, wisdom-file).
  - the section named by [global]'s "hardware =" -> HARDWARE_ADVANCED_SETTINGS
    keyed by that section's own "device =" value (hackrf/rx888/rtlsdr/
    sig_gen have real catalogs; anything else falls back to a generic
    description/serial/samprate list).
  - every other section (a channel/preset group) -> CHANNEL_ADVANCED_SETTINGS:
    the per-channel tuning keys ka9q-radio's own `control` program exposes
    via single-letter hotkeys (AGC threshold/recovery-rate/hang-time/
    headroom, PLL bandwidth, squelch open/close/tail, de-emphasis,
    kaiser-beta, filter2, encoding, bitrate, status update interval,
    output buffering).
All of these are keys this editor's plain "add a key" flow already
accepted; the catalogs just supply a name, a documented default, and a
one-line explanation instead of requiring the operator to already know
them. Picking one offers a choice of adding it live (active key=value) or
as a commented-out note, applied straight to the in-memory editor copy
like any other 'a'/'c' edit -- it shows up in the tree immediately and
only reaches disk (in /tmp) via the usual 'w' Write.

Usage:
    scripts/ka9q-radio-builder

Requires the "textual" Python package -- see KA9Q-DEPLOYMENT.md's
"Interactive alternative" section for how to install it on Debian/Ubuntu.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

